## 1. The Diagnostic Framework

**Definition 1: Mental Illness (as Maladaptive Pattern)**

Let \( \mathcal{M}_i \) denote mental illness:

\[
\mathcal{M}_i := 
\begin{array}{l}
\text{A pattern of behavior, thinking, or relating} \\
\text{That causes significant dysfunction, distress, or harm} \\
\text{To the individual or others} \\
\text{And is not grounded in reality}
\end{array}
\]

**Definition 2: The Status Quo Paradigm**

Let \( \mathcal{S}_q \) denote the status quo paradigm:

\[
\mathcal{S}_q := 
\begin{array}{l}
\text{The dominant framework of thought and practice} \\
\text{Characterized by:} \\
\quad \text{Nihilism's will to power} \\
\quad \text{Machiavellianism} \\
\quad \text{The law of the jungle} \\
\quad \text{Egotism} \\
\quad \text{Self-grounding logic}
\end{array}
\]

**Theorem 1: The Status Quo Paradigm Exhibits Criteria for Mental Illness**

\[
\mathcal{S}_q \to \mathcal{M}_i
\]

